# _headers = Security Headers Policy
#
# @summary
# Hardening headers applied to every response. The app loads nothing but its
# own self-hosted files, so the Content-Security-Policy stays strict, opening
# only what a build's own Firebase project needs. The build fills in three
# placeholders from its project (deploy-files.ts): %FIREBASE_CONNECT% adds
# the project's addresses to connect-src, %FIREBASE_FRAME% sets frame-src to
# the app itself, for the sign-in page Netlify passes through, plus
# reCAPTCHA's frames when App Check is on, and %FIREBASE_SCRIPT% adds the
# helper script Google sign-in loads from apis.google.com, plus reCAPTCHA's
# script then. With App Check on, connect-src also gains reCAPTCHA. A build
# without a project, such as a test build, adds nothing and keeps frame-src
# at 'none'.
#
# default-src 'self' is the fallback every unlisted fetch directive inherits,
# such as the one for fonts, so those need no lines of their own. The
# directives listed either need a different value than 'self', are never
# covered by default-src at all, such as frame-ancestors, or just state the
# default for clarity, such as script-src. img-src adds blob: for event photos,
# which are decrypted on the device and shown, and shrunk before they're
# shared, from temporary addresses only this page can make. style-src needs no
# 'unsafe-inline': the build ships no inline styles, and a style React sets
# later through the DOM isn't restricted by the policy.
#
# Strict-Transport-Security keeps browsers on HTTPS for two years across every
# subdomain, leaving out preload, which commits the domain to the browsers'
# built-in HTTPS list and is slow to undo. Referrer-Policy sends a referrer
# only to this same origin. Permissions-Policy turns off powerful browser
# features the app doesn't use yet, such as the camera, and allows
# geolocation to this origin alone, for sharing a location on an event day.
#
# @author z4nta0 <https://github.com/z4nta0>

# What: Security Headers Rule. Why: Every response should refuse framing, sniffing, plain HTTP, referrer leaks to other sites, unused browser features, and anything loaded from another origin but its own Firebase project. How: This applies the Content-Security-Policy, its project's placeholders filled in by the build, the permissions and referrer policies, HSTS, a nosniff content type, and a deny on framing to every path.
/*
	Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; connect-src 'self'%FIREBASE_CONNECT%; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; frame-src %FIREBASE_FRAME%; img-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; script-src 'self'%FIREBASE_SCRIPT%; style-src 'self'
	Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), autoplay=(), browsing-topics=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), encrypted-media=(), fullscreen=(), geolocation=(self), gyroscope=(), hid=(), idle-detection=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), midi=(), payment=(), picture-in-picture=(), publickey-credentials-get=(), screen-wake-lock=(), serial=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
	Referrer-Policy: same-origin
	Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=63072000; includeSubDomains
	X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
	X-Frame-Options: DENY



# What: Manifest Content Type Rule. Why: Netlify serves a .webmanifest file as application/octet-stream, which Chrome tolerates but Firefox and some install paths reject, as ease-my-life found. How: This sends the manifest with its own type.
/manifest.webmanifest
	Content-Type: application/manifest+json
